  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 231 AM MDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nOn tuesday, upper-level southwesterly flow continues over the\narea as we are behind a ridge to the east and in front of a deep\ntrough system to the southwest. This will allow a shortwave\ntrough to move into the area in the afternoon with a possible\nsurface low pressure system to the west. This would lead to \nafternoon showers north of I-70 and weak thunderstorms in the \nsoutheast portions of the forecast area as upslope low level \nmoisture will be fed in from the southeast. However, there is\nlow confidence (30%) that the surface low will form and showers\nto occur. \n\nWednesday, the upper-level pattern path remains similar as the\nupper-level trough moves closer and into the Four Corners\nregion. However, uncertainty regarding the timing of the upper-\nlevel trough starts to grow throughout the period. This will\nlead into morning showers and potential strong and low topped\nthunderstorms in the afternoon. The main driving factors for\nthunderstorms are potential CAPE values above 500 J/kg and a\nstrong surface to 3 km wind shear profile. The main potential \nhazard will be strong winds up to 50 mph. While confidence for\nshowers are medium (50-65%), confidence for severe weather are\nlower (at 25%).\n\nThursday, the upper-level pattern will start to change from\nsouthwesterly flow to northwesterly as the trough moves through\nthe area. Timing of the trough is still inconsistent and becomes\neven more variable. Although, a dry cold front is forecast to\npass through and push any remaining showers to the northeast. If\nthe trough speeds up, it could pass through the early morning\nhours and have drier conditions. If the trough slows down, it\nwill then allow more time for precipitation to accumulate and \nhave warmer afternoon temperatures. \n\nFriday, as the upper-level troughs moves further northeast, it\nis likely to merge with a secondary upper-level trough to the\nnorth. This could lead to cooler temperatures than expected with\ncalm winds from the north as an additional cold front could pass\nthrough.",
